t just about the big-budget  Triple A games independent titles and  breakthrough actor also recognized at  the ceremony inside the eerie puzzle  platformer developed by indie studio  play dead took home four Awards on the  night more than any other title and Best  British game went to over cooked which  was made by a two-man team meanwhile  that dragon cancer won the prize for  game innovation it deals with  bereavement and was inspired by the  creator's own experiences following the  loss of their four-year-old son to  cancer our life with Joel was hard but  it was really beautiful  and so when we thought about creating a  game that would become the only legacy  he would have the legacy we chose and he  could not we created a game that was  hard to play but we believed it was  beautiful I don't think that we're the  first were certainly not the first but  we're among a class of games that are  telling more personal stories that  happened to trigger emotions like  empathy and in dealing with grief in  dealing with sorrow and and having to  bear witness to those things in life  that aren't always fun and they aren't  always the pastimes with the  things that change us the most this  year's Baptists featured a real mix of  different games with success coming to  companies both big and small so even if  awards don't necessarily make much  difference to all developers at least  the ceremony provides an opportunity to  showcase the variety of the industry  Chris Creegan Sky News now we're gonna  step away from BAFTA glory for just a  minute to round up what else has been  going on in the world of tech this week  members of the public have started  testing a driverless vehicle in London a  prototype shuttle pod has been moving  around pedestrianised areas in Greenwich  with no brake pedal or steering wheel  the temporary trial is part of a project  to see how people respond to the  experience scientists at the University  of Manchester have discovered a way to  sieve seawater and turn it into drinking  water using a membrane made of graphene  researchers were able to remove salt  from the water and make it safe to drink  AOL confirmed its merging with Yahoo the  two internet firms will form a new joint  company called oath his parent company  will be Verizon one of America's biggest  communications firm it's thought nearly  half of UK households are frustrated  with poor broadband consumer group which  surveyed over 2000 households with 46  percent reporting problems with their  broadband based on the population that  equals around 12.5 million households  now tonight is all about the BAFTAs but  this whole week has been a key one for  the British games industry because it's  also been London Games Festival a little  earlier I managed to have a quick chat  with the woman at the top of the UK's  interactive entertainment trade body and  I began by asking her if people still  underestimate the video games industry I  think people do underestimate it I mean  we're faster growing than film and music  globally consumers spend over 4 billion  pounds in 2016 and gains 1 in 3 people  play 2 billion players worldwide so  we're absolutely massive economic  powerhouse but we're a huge cultural  powerhouse what do you think needs to  change so that video games get the same  recognition as their movie and music  counterparts I mean I think some people  would argue that we do have the  recognition and again BAFTA as a charity  recognizing games just as much as they  recognize film and TVs great sign but I  think it's around sometimes it's the way  that the media communicates about games  particularly to parents and to teachers  who might not consider using games in  the classroom or who might not you know  know about the different kinds of  fantastic games that they can play  together as a family  I think Pokemon Gogh actually did which  is one of the nominated games tonight  that did actually change a lot of  perceptions with parents and with the  media but I think you know generally  there needs to be more kind of cultural  support for games you see the film  industry the TV industry benefits from  public funding to help new ideas new  directors new developers could benefit  from that if we had that kind of support  as well do you think people who don't  play games just don't get it  I think it's a weird one I think there's  a real cultural bias still with games  and and I think if you say are you a  gamer most people will say no I say I  don't like the term gamer but I play  games just as much as I read books I  watch films I watch TV so I think you  know I think it's changing but I think  some sometimes people assume that games  are just for kids in fact you know  people who play games are in the average  ages in their mid-30s so London games  first of all why does the capital need a  Games Festival you know the UK is the  birthplace in the cradle of the games  industry with the sixth largest consumer  market in the world and we're really the  home of development in Europe London has  this real kind of hotbed of talent we  also have the best university courses we  have such an amazing creative economy  with the film industry TV advertising  architecture so this huge creativity  London and the Games Festival really  does try and shine that international  spotlight on London as a gateway to the  rest of the UK and indeed to Europe and  in a post brexit well what's going to  change I'm looking forward to the games  that are going to come out around  breakfast I think for us it's really  about explaining to government and  really pushing in negotiations for a  real friction-free immigration system we  need to be able to hire the best talent  globally  wherever they are if anyone who didn't  get why you laughed about games coming  out around brexit time would you explain  that I'm really games are really good at  picking up on issues and games can be  comedy they can be parodies and game  developers and the 